"""E10 (r), retrospective, zero compute (no training): our race (N1), the static window and the drive-vs-decay
counts of De Melo Costa et al. (arXiv 2610.02344v1; code github.com/jose-melo/drive-vs-decay @ 3176215, 2026-10-01)
scored from inputs alone on their public Tabular-JEPA outcomes. Everything here is retrospective: their outcomes
were public before this script was written. No prediction reads an outcome column.

Their setting (tabular/experiment_1.py, reproduce_tabular.sh @ 3176215): linear encoder x -> LayerNorm(W (m1*x) + b),
d = 64, kaiming_normal(a = sqrt 5) W, zero b; linear predictor Wp (64 x 64, no bias, no residual) ~ N(0, alpha^2);
EMA target encoder on m2*x (momentum 0.996 -> 1); loss MSE; AdamW lr 1e-3, wd 1e-5 on encoder and predictor, cosine
to 0.01 lr, clip 1.0, batch 256 (drop_last), 30 epochs in the script (the paper says 40); masks: per sample an
independent random subset of k1 = max(1, int(n ctx)) context and k2 = max(1, int(n tgt)) target features,
tgt = min(0.5, max(0.1, 1 - ctx)), overlap allowed; data: stratified 80/10/10 split (random_state = seed),
mean imputation, per-column min-max scaling fitted on train. Grid: aloi (n = 128), helena (27), jannis (54);
alpha in {1e-4, 1e-3, 0.01, 0.1, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10}; ctx in {0.001, 0.01, 0.1, ..., 0.8}; seeds 42, 123, 456.

Inputs per row (all from data and hyperparameters; nothing from the run):
  statistics of the min-max-scaled train split, replicated from their loaders and the OpenML ids of their download
  script (aloi 1592, helena 41169, jannis 41168). Exact masked moments for independent random-subset masks with
  G_ii = k1/n, G_ij = k1 (k1 - 1)/(n (n - 1)):
    centred (primary; the encoder has a bias, and their code centres):  S11 = G o M - (k1/n)^2 mu mu^T,
                                                                       S21 = (k1 k2/n^2) (M - mu mu^T);
    uncentred (theory definition, sensitivity '_unc'):                 S11 = G o M,  S21 = (k1 k2/n^2) M;
  M = E[x x^T], mu = E[x] over the train split. The target density k2/n sits in S21 (their code puts it in gamma).
  W0, P0: the torch draws of their constructors under torch.manual_seed(seed + 1) (Linear(n, 64) reset, kaiming,
  Linear(64, 64) reset, normal_(std = alpha)); seed + 1 is inferred from their CSV (cells with the same k1 have
  identical outcomes, so each cell was a fresh run with config_id = 1). '_altP' redraws P0 independently.
  lam = wd = 1e-5 in units where the GD step is lr (the decoupled decay per step lr wd matches); horizon
  t_end = sum of the cosine lr over the run, so the predictor's own decay over the run is exp(-wd t_end) > 0.9999.
Criteria (linearised at W = 0 with the predictor at P0, no LayerNorm, no bias, as in both theories):
  window: data modes u_k (eigenvectors of S11, a_k), c_k = u_k^T sym(S21) u_k; direction W = w u_k^T obeys
    w' = A_k w, A_k = c_k P^T - a_k P^T P - lam (the cross-mode terms of S21 in the S11 basis are dropped);
    mode k is open iff max Re eig(A_k) > 0; count = min(#open k, span of their unstable eigenvectors, d).
  race (N1 on this block reduction): mode k escapes iff it is open at P0 and, with P(t) = exp(-lam t) P0 and
    F(t) = int max Re eig(A_k(t)), log|W0 u_k| + F(t) clears the capture amplitude (1/2) log(lam |p| / |c_k - p a_k|)
    (p = v^H P v on the top unstable eigenvector v, drive sign(p)(c_k - p a_k) > 0) before it leaves the window,
    t <= t_end. Same count rule. Here the predictor barely decays and W0 = O(1) lies far above the capture scale, so
    race = window is an implementation consistency check, not a test; a tie with the window is not a win.
  window_adamw: the same window with lam = 0 (theory/ext_A_adamw.md A-i: under AdamW the window 0 < p < c/s loses
    its weight-decay dependence near collapse).
  dvd, dvd_nowd, dvd_signed, dvd_sorted, dvd_pq, dvd_pq_signed, dvd_exact: as in code/baselines/dvd_mu.py
    (their r_tight, Eq. 51-52 and variants, with the weight-decay floor lam); dvd_exact only for helena (the
    Kronecker eigenproblem is 1728^2 there, 3456^2 and 8192^2 for jannis and aloi, beyond the CPU budget).
    dvd_exact is also the exact, non-decoupled form of our window in the lam_P -> 0 limit (same linear operator,
    same span count), so window vs dvd_exact on helena measures the error of the block reduction above.
  dvd_final_theirs: their recorded tjepa/rc_r_tight_abs, a reference only: final-checkpoint values (their App. C.7)
    computed by code that is not in the public repository, so not from inputs alone.
Measured quantity: their CSV has no thresholded singular-value count; the closest measured rank is tjepa/effective_rank
  (ERank, exp-entropy of the normalised singular values of a batch of up to 256 embeddings; rc_erank_repr agrees).
  All counts are scored against ERank. Both papers say a count is not an ERank prediction (their Prop. C.4: r_tight
  is an upper bound), so Spearman is the primary statistic and MAE, over and exceed are reported beside it.
Tab. 16 (T-JEPA transformer on jannis): a 16-layer transformer predictor, no weight decay; neither criterion can be
  computed faithfully from inputs for it. Reported: their own epoch-0 mu_50 against the outcome, and a labelled
  linear analogue (jannis statistics, 64 x 64 Gaussian P0 at alpha, lam = 0), with the structural fact that at
  lam = 0 every count of the form c q > a p (p = s^2, q = s) is non-increasing in alpha.
Usage: python3 code/e10_retro.py <drive-vs-decay checkout @ 3176215> <OpenML cache dir> results/e10
Inputs: results/tabular_phase_grid/full_sweep_per_run.csv, results/tabular_phase_grid/transformer_grid_jannis.csv
  (their repository); OpenML 1592, 41169, 41168 via sklearn.datasets.fetch_openml (cached in the given directory).
